<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rama is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god, the seventh avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and the hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>. In the epic he is a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in Kosala, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> line, who is exiled to the forest, rescues his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> from Ravana, the demon king of Lanka, and returns to rule in an ideal reign. His story is retold across South and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and his worship is central to Vaishnava devotion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rama.jpg" alt="Rama" width="320" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gita Press Gorakhpur · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rama_in_Adhyatma_Ramayana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Rama is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> with two contextual meanings. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharva Veda</a> it means dark or black, and in other Vedic texts pleasing, delightful or beautiful. The primary source of his life is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, composed by the sage Valmiki, which exists in many versions across the regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts place Rama in the Treta Yuga, which their authors estimate to have existed before about 5000 BCE. The archaeologist H. D. Sankalia calls this estimate pure speculation and suggests that the incidents of the story could have taken place between 1,500 and 700 BCE. Jain and Buddhist texts also tell the story, calling him Pauma or Padma in Jain accounts, with details that differ from the Hindu versions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>According to Valmiki, Rama was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, capital of Kosala, to Dasaratha and his first wife Kausalya, and had the half-brothers Lakshmana, Bharata and Shatrughna. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> and spent fourteen years in forest exile, during which Sita was kidnapped by the demon-king Ravana. After the war he returned to Ayodhya and was crowned, and his rule, Rama rajya, is described as just and fair. Many believe that people lit lamps for his return, which links the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a> with him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> hold Rama to be a complete avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and some regard him as the ultimate Brahman. He is also viewed as the ideal man. By Hindu belief this avatar does not display Vishnu's divine powers and lives as a human. Scholars such as Paula Richman note that there are hundreds of versions of his story, none the single original, each valid in its local tradition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> has been retold in most major Indian languages, among them Kambar's Tamil Ramavataram in the 12th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krittibas-ojha">Krittibas Ojha</a>'s Bengali version in the 15th and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>'s Awadhi Ramacharitamanasa in the 16th. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>'s Raghuvamsa suggests that the legend was well established by the 4th or 5th century CE. The Ramanandi Sampradaya, founded by the 14th-century poet-saint Ramananda, became the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monastic community in modern times.</p><p>The story arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> in the 1st millennium CE, and its influence grew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. It inspired Madhubani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> paintings and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> performances held each autumn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name joins the Gurjaras, a people and later a social group widely present in the region, whose origins are debated, with the title pratihara, ‘doorkeeper’; the dynasty’s own Gwalior inscription traced its descent from Lakshmana, who served as doorkeeper to his brother <b>Rama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between them stood officials titled Rakai or Samget, landed gentry who ruled a watak of several villages and passed the king's orders to the <b>Rama</b>, the village heads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The major traditions are devoted to Vishnu, who descends to earth in incarnations (avatars) such as <b>Rama</b> and Krishna; to Shiva, the ascetic and the lord of creation and destruction; and to the Goddess, Devi, in forms such as Durga, Kali, Lakshmi and Parvati.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…longest poems in the world, tells of the war between two branches of a royal family and contains the Bhagavad Gita, the dialogue between the warrior Arjuna and the god Krishna; the Ramayana, attributed to the sage Valmiki, tells the story of Prince <b>Rama</b> and his wife Sita.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Tulsidas recorded in the Ramcharitmanas itself that he began the poem at Ayodhya in 1574, on the festival of <b>Rama</b>’s birth; the Ramlila plays still staged across northern India each autumn are based on its verses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Jain versions of the epic it is Lakshmana, not <b>Rama</b>, who kills Ravana, and Jain texts hold that Ravana will become a future Tirthankara.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
